## FINDINGS

1: docs/02-game-rules.md — "2. Apply moves **in request order**: movement = teleport to `path.at(-1)` (validation proved the path exists, no step simulation needed)." — Whole-bundle validation (step 1) checks every path against the **pre-move** unit positions, but movements are then applied as teleports in request order. Two units in the same bundle can therefore both legally target the same empty tile (empty at validation time) and end up stacked on one tile, or be blocked from ending on an ally's origin even though that ally vacates it. No rule resolves a same-tile collision, so one agent may allow the stack/last-write-wins while another rejects converging paths — divergent, unchecked post-bundle state.

2: docs/05-frontend.md — "Totals card (games, win rate big numerals) + the per-difficulty table." — "difficulty" is never defined in any of the six read docs (no config field, no game-creation input, no persona→difficulty map; `/api/meta` is fetched only for "persona labels + cap"). An agent must invent the difficulty axis and how games are bucketed, so the stats table and its tests are ungrounded and inconsistent across implementations.

3: docs/03-board-generation.md — "The simple-path property alone structurally prevents spirals, loops and floods" vs "each river tile has exactly 2 river/bridge neighbors except the two edge tiles, which have 1." — A simple (non-repeating) path can still orthogonally touch itself, giving a river tile three neighbors and violating invariant 1. The doc overstates the guarantee, so an agent may skip the neighbor-count check or under-provision the 3-seed/3-brief retry budget, producing boards that fail invariant 1 or fall back to the hardcoded template more often than implied.

## AMBIGUITIES

1: docs/02-game-rules.md — "2. Apply moves in request order: movement = teleport to path.at(-1) ... 3. Attack: ... Manhattan distance ... from the ATTACKER'S FINAL TILE must be in range. ... 4. Counter — if the target survived ... ⇒ the target deals ... back. One counter per attack; the counter can kill." — Interpretation A: attacks resolve sequentially per bundle entry (a unit killed by a counter never makes its own later attack; overkill is possible). Interpretation B: all attacks resolve simultaneously from pre-attack HP (a countered-killed unit still deals its damage). Also unresolved: whether all movements precede all attacks or each move is move-then-action. Combat results (who dies) differ between the two.

2: docs/02-game-rules.md — "export const SIZE = 15; // boardSize also lives in config; engine takes size as an argument — tests parametrize it" vs docs/03-board-generation.md "on a 15x15 grid", docs/04-ai-integration.md "on a 15x15 grid", docs/05-frontend.md "Board = CSS grid, `15×15`". — Interpretation A: board size is fixed at 15 everywhere (config's boardSize mirrors SIZE). Interpretation B: board size is config-driven (engine is size-parametrized) but the generator, AI coordinates, and frontend grid hardcode 15 with no doc binding them to config.boardSize — if config.boardSize ever differs from 15 the three layers silently disagree.

3: docs/04-ai-integration.md — "zod: { moves: [{ unitId: string, path: [x,y][], action: {type:"attack"|"heal"|"wait", targetId?: string} }] }" vs docs/02-game-rules.md (attack requires a target, heal requires a target, wait has none; the rejection catalogue lists no message for a missing/unexpected targetId). — Interpretation A: the engine rejects attack-without-target and wait-with-target as illegal moves. Interpretation B: the engine tolerates them (attack with no target is a no-op; wait ignores a target). Schema-valid but semantically off LLM replies are handled differently by different agents.

## CONTRADICTIONS

1: docs/03-board-generation.md — "front = the row facing the river (player row 13, enemy row 0); back = the row farther from the center (player 14, enemy 1)" vs docs/03-board-generation.md — its own definitions "front = the row facing the river ... back = the row farther from the center" (for the enemy on the top rows 0–1, row 1 faces the river and row 0 is farther from center, so the parenthetical's enemy rows are the reverse of the definition), and vs docs/04-ai-integration.md's worked example, which places enemy front units (swords/axes/spear-1) at y=1 and back units (spear-2/archers/mage/healer) at y=0. The doc tells agents to put enemy melee on row 0 while its own definition and the 04 example put them on row 1 — enemy spawn layout is specified two opposite ways.

## VERDICT

1. The enemy spawn-row contradiction (docs/03-board-generation.md) is the most damaging: it specifies enemy front/back rows two opposite ways, so agents will build boards that the AI prompt and movement rules don't match.
2. Whole-bundle validation against pre-move state plus teleport application lets two units converge on one tile with no collision rule (docs/02-game-rules.md), so agents produce different, inconsistent post-bundle states.
3. Attack/counter resolution order inside a bundle is unspecified (docs/02-game-rules.md), so agents disagree on whether a unit killed by a counter still deals damage, changing combat outcomes.